{
  "book": "LUK",
  "chapter": 10,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-03082f107c278934": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-768",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The exhortation to diligence in their work, alluded to in the last note, is repeated in a different form in this text. As salutations are tedious and frequent, so Oriental etiquette requires much visiting from house to house. This consumes time and takes attention from business;…"
      },
      "c-69d3236bde51b019": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-770",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This was a favorite application for wounds in ancient surgery. It was considered a sovereign remedy, especially for wounds produced by violence; wool, lint, or pounded olive being first laid upon the wound. The wine was supposed to cleanse, and the oil to soothe and heal. The two…"
      },
      "c-c9faf58c26ec4839": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-769",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Jews gave a very narrow definition of the word “neighbor.” The robbing interpreted it to mean only those who were of the Jewish people. The Gentiles were not considered neighbors."
      },
      "c-e5fc2ea2dec4e839": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-767",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This was not designed to forbid them from exercising the usual courtesies of life, in giving a mere salaam to those they might meet, as is evident from the very next verse, and also from Matthew 10:12-13. They were, doubtless, allowed to give the usual salutation of “Peace!” See…"
      }
    },
